Interview Questions & Answers

Q1. What is the Snowflake Data Marketplace and how does it differ from direct data sharing?

The Data Marketplace is a public catalog within Snowsight where users can browse and import third-party data shares. The key differences from direct sharing are:

Direct sharing is private and account-to-account, while the marketplace is public and open to all consumers
The marketplace has a browsable catalog — users don't need to know the provider in advance
Direct sharing requires manual setup by both parties, while marketplace listings use a guided wizard

However, both use the exact same underlying Share technology — the marketplace is essentially a discovery and distribution layer built on top of direct sharing.


Q2. Who can be a Data Consumer and who can be a Data Provider on the marketplace?


Data Consumer — ALL Snowflake accounts can be data consumers by default. No setup or application is required.
Data Provider — accounts must apply to become a Snowflake partner by completing a form accessible at the bottom of the marketplace page in the "Interested in becoming a data provider" section. Not all accounts are automatically providers.



Q3. What is the difference between a Standard and Personalized listing?

Standard Listing:

Any account can click Get and instantly receive access
No approval process — out-of-the-box access
Typically free
Best for broadly available public datasets

Personalized Listing:

Consumer must click Request and fill out a form
Provider reviews and manually processes each request
Can include a cost/charge for data access — used for monetization
Establishes a relationship between provider and consumer
Best for curated, customized, or commercial datasets



Q4. What happens when a consumer clicks "Get" on a standard marketplace listing?

A simple wizard appears that allows the consumer to:

Choose a name for the new database
Add additional roles (beyond ACCOUNTADMIN) that will have access to the shared data (e.g. SYSADMIN)

Once completed, the database is created instantly from the share — just like a direct share import. Most listings also auto-generate a sample worksheet with example queries so the consumer can immediately explore the data.


Q5. How does the Data Marketplace relate to data monetization?

The marketplace enables data monetization primarily through Personalized Listings. Providers can:

Require consumers to submit a request with their intended use and contact details
Establish a commercial relationship before granting access
Charge a fee for access to their datasets

Standard listings can also be monetized but are more commonly used for freely available public data. The marketplace essentially gives data providers a storefront for their data products.


Q6. What technology powers the Data Marketplace behind the scenes?

The Data Marketplace uses the exact same Share technology as direct account-to-account data sharing. When a consumer imports a marketplace listing, Snowflake creates a database from a share object in the same way as a direct share — no data is physically moved or copied. The marketplace is purely a discovery, distribution, and access management layer built on top of the existing sharing infrastructure.


Q7. Where is the Data Marketplace accessible and what are its key benefits?

The Data Marketplace is accessible only within Snowsight (Snowflake's web-based UI). Key benefits:

Access live, real-time third-party data — not static snapshots or exports
Seamlessly join third-party data with your own Snowflake data
No ETL pipelines needed — data is available instantly via share
Auto-generated sample worksheets make it easy to explore new datasets
Consumers pay only for the compute used to query the data



